        paired with this one, and that is not what Share was being asked for.</string>
    <string name="share_refused_not_text">There was no text in what was shared, so Sharepaste
        sent nothing. It syncs text — share a link, a note or a code.</string>

    <!--
      Pending, surfaced. Sync is foreground-only, so an Offer made with no
      connection waits for the next time the app is opened; a count nobody can
      see is a count nobody comes back for.

      The count is drawn beside this sentence as a readout rather than written
      into it, which is why there is no `%1$d` here: on the History the number
      is the thing being reported and the sentence is what it means.
    -->
    <plurals name="pending_count">
        <item quantity="one">Entry is waiting for the Relay. Sharepaste sends it while this
            app is open.</item>
        <item quantity="other">Entries are waiting for the Relay. Sharepaste sends them
            while this app is open.</item>
    </plurals>

    <!--
      The same queue on a Pairing card, where it has no room for a readout and
      has to carry its own count. This is the only surface that shows a queue
      belonging to a Pairing the phone switched away from.
    -->
    <plurals name="pairings_pending">
        <item quantity="one">%1$d ENTRY WAITING FOR THE RELAY · SENT WHILE THIS APP IS
            OPEN</item>
        <item quantity="other">%1$d ENTRIES WAITING FOR THE RELAY · SENT WHILE THIS APP IS
            OPEN</item>
    </plurals>

    <!--
      The one honest consequence of ADR 0007, in the app's own voice and on the
      screen rather than in a release note. It is the single most surprising
      thing about how this app works, and a person who does not know it will
      read it as a bug.

      It is pinned chrome, not a list item: clipped to one line until asked and
      verbatim once opened. A fact that scrolls away is a fact the puzzled
      person never reaches, and this is the one they are puzzled about.
    -->
    <string name="foreground_only_pinned">NOTHING ARRIVES WHILE THIS IS CLOSED</string>
    <string name="foreground_only_why">WHY \u25B8</string>
    <string name="foreground_only_close">\u25B4 CLOSE</string>
    <!--
      What the band's tap does, for a screen reader. The chip says `WHY ▸`,
      which is an affordance rather than a sentence, and the whole band is the
      target.
    -->
    <string name="foreground_only_why_action">Show why nothing arrives while this is
        closed</string>
    <string name="foreground_only_note">Sharepaste only looks for new Entries while this app is
        open. It does no work in the background — that is deliberate, and it is why nothing here
        drains your battery or reads your clipboard behind your back. Something you copied on
        the computer an hour ago arrives the moment you open Sharepaste, not before.</string>
    <!-- The same fact as four things that are not happening. -->
    <string name="foreground_only_tag_sync">NO BACKGROUND SYNC</string>
    <string name="foreground_only_tag_notification">NO NEW-ENTRY NOTIFICATION</string>
    <string name="foreground_only_tag_watching">NO CLIPBOARD WATCHING</string>
    <string name="foreground_only_tag_counterparty">ONE COUNTERPARTY: YOUR RELAY</string>

    <!--
      Contact, in the phone's terms, and permanently on screen rather than only
      when degraded. The desktop's rule is the other way round (ADR 0002); a
      phone is out of contact almost always, so hiding the nominal reading would
      leave the band meaning "something is wrong" every time it appeared.
    -->
    <string name="contact_looking">CHECKING FOR NEW ENTRIES\u2026</string>
    <string name="contact_online">IN CONTACT WITH THE RELAY</string>
    <string name="contact_offline">NOT IN CONTACT · NOMINAL</string>
    <string name="contact_resting">RESTING · NOT LOOKING WHILE CLOSED</string>
    <!--
      A Pairing that is not the Active one. Distinct from `contact_resting`,
      which is the *phone* put down: this one is idle on a phone that is wide
      awake, and it is nominal. A Pairing nobody asked to connect is resting,
      not faulty.
    -->
    <string name="contact_not_active">RESTING · NOT SYNCING THIS PAIRING</string>
    <!--
      The one true fault, and the only readout that is a whole sentence: no
      amount of waiting fixes a revoked token, so this one has to say what to do
      about it rather than report a state.
    -->
    <string name="contact_refused_short">RELAY REFUSED THIS PHONE</string>
    <string name="contact_refused">The Relay no longer recognises this phone. It has probably
        been unpaired from your computer; pair it again to start over.</string>
    <string name="contact_pair_again">PAIR THIS PHONE AGAIN</string>

    <!--
      ===================================================================
      The Settings Screen: the Pairings, what can be done to one, and the
      one setting a phone has of its own. Exactly one Pairing is Active —
      that is what this phone syncs, and it survives a restart. Any may be
      Viewed, which changes nothing and is forgotten when the app is put
      down (CONTEXT.md).

      The keys still say `pairings_` and the screen now says SETTINGS. That
      is deliberate and matches `Screen.Pairings`, which also stays: this is
      an information-architecture change, not a routing one, and renaming
      the keys would churn every reference to make a screen title agree with
      a symbol nobody reads.
      ===================================================================
    -->
    <string name="pairings_title">SETTINGS</string>
    <string name="pairings_open">Settings</string>
    <string name="pairings_back">Back</string>
    <string name="pairings_active_badge">SYNCING</string>
    <string name="pairings_viewed_badge">SHOWING</string>
    <!-- %1$s is the Device Label this phone chose when it paired. -->
    <string name="pairings_this_phone">This phone here: %1$s</string>
    <string name="pairings_view">SHOW ITS ENTRIES</string>
    <string name="pairings_use">SYNC THIS ONE</string>
    <string name="pairings_clear_history">CLEAR HISTORY</string>
    <string name="pairings_forget">FORGET</string>
    <string name="pairings_cancel">KEEP IT</string>

    <!--
      The divergence, admitted. Without this the History shows one Pairing's
      Entries while the phone syncs another, and a list that is quietly frozen
      looks exactly like a list that is up to date. %1$s is the Pairing on
      screen, %2$s the one being synced.
    -->
    <string name="pairing_diverged">You are looking at %1$s, but this phone is syncing %2$s.
        Nothing here is being kept up to date, and anything you offer goes to %2$s.</string>
    <string name="pairing_diverged_use">SYNC THIS ONE INSTEAD</string>

    <!--
      The two erasures name the User and the Relay rather than the heading: two
      Pairings can share a name, and neither of these can be undone. %1$s is
      "<user> @ <relay host>". Both are answered inside the card they belong to
      rather than in a dialog, so the scope stays on screen while the choice is
      being made.
    -->
    <string name="pairings_confirm_badge">CANNOT BE UNDONE</string>
    <string name="pairings_clear_confirm">Erase every Entry of %1$s, on the Relay and on every
